--TEST--
`Core\Arr::range` over a span wider than `[limits] memory` is stopped inside the loop, so the list it was building never reaches the program — `rule:programs/memory-priority`, `rule:errors/on-limit`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// The loop is native: between two entries the request passes no statement
// boundary and calls no member, so the ask `crates/nvs-stdlib/src/arr.rs` makes
// per entry is the only thing in the loop that reads the balance. The length is
// the bounds', and a program that arrived with a request writes those.

// Four million entries is sixty-four megabytes, four times the ceiling below,
// so the bound is unreachable in a run this case passes and a red case fails
// rather than taking the suite's host with it.

// `--EXPECT--` is exact, so this line being the whole of stdout is what asserts
// the loop was stopped where it grew: the `echo` under it is reached only by a
// call that came back with a list.
echo "before\n";
array<int> $everything = Core\Arr::range(1, 4000000);
echo "kept ", Core\Arr::count($everything), "\n";
--FILE nvs.toml--
# The ceiling the loop above crosses, and nothing else.
[limits]
memory = "16M"
--EXPECT--
before
--EXPECTF-ERROR--
FATAL: the request exceeded its memory limit — %d bytes held against a ceiling of 16777216, of which 1048576 is reserved for the limit handler, when an allocation of %d bytes was refused
